<Layout
  title="5G and IoT Supply Chain Analysis"
  description="Excerpt: a research synthesis on US-China trade and security dynamics in 5G and IoT, originally written for the Hudson Institute."
>
  <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">
    Writing Sample
  </p>
  <h1 class="mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
    5G and IoT: Supply Chain Analysis, Security Implications, and Their Impact on US-China Trade
  </h1>
  <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">Hudson Institute · August 2019</p>

  <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
    The following excerpts are drawn from a longer research synthesis mapping the security, trade,
    and supply-chain dynamics of the US-China race for 5G and IoT dominance, pulling together patent
    filings, breaking news, and legislation to inform strategy.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-10 space-y-10">
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
        Policy Divergence Among Allies
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        This excerpt traces how the same security concerns about Huawei produced opposite policy
        outcomes in Australia and the UK, by reading each country's decision against its own
        political history with the company.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-4 border-l-4 border-neutral-300 pl-5 text-neutral-700 dark:border-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        <p>
          Of the Five Eyes alliance, New Zealand, Australia, and the United States are the only
          members which have outright banned Huawei from their 5G infrastructure, although recent
          events concerning the United States have put the ban into jeopardy. Australia led the
          charge with banning Huawei in 2018 following an investigation into the security of their
          devices. Australia then shared the information with the United States and other countries,
          urging them to tighten their 5G supply chain. The difference in attitude between Australia
          and the UK can be traced to their historical relationship with the Chinese company. Since
          2012, Australia has been wary of the company and prevented them from participating in the
          National Broadband Network project. This has been attributed to China's growing influence
          in Australia, as it has had issues with China's political influence in the past, including
          donations to particular election campaigns. In addition to the 5G ban, Australia also
          recently introduced legislation to combat foreign influence in politics.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the other hand, the UK has been using Huawei equipment in its major networks, Vodafone
          and BT Group, for years, and have even cooperated with Huawei to create a cybersecurity
          evaluation center in order to ease security concerns.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
        Legislative Response in Congress
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        This excerpt tracks a specific bipartisan bill back to the executive action and diplomatic
        meeting that prompted it, then weighs the pushback it drew from industry, the kind of
        tracing-intent-through-text that a legislative landscape map depends on.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-4 border-l-4 border-neutral-300 pl-5 text-neutral-700 dark:border-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
        <p>
          Legislators are also keeping in mind the risks of China leading in the 5G domain. Recently
          several bills have been introduced to minimize the risk of Chinese telecom to the United
          States. "The Defending America's 5G Future Act" is being introduced by Sens. Marco Rubio
          (R-Fla.), Mitt Romney (R-Utah), Mark Warner (D-Va.) and Richard Blumenthal (D-Conn.) in the
          Senate, and its companion bill is being introduced by Representatives Mike Gallagher
          (R-Wisconsin), Jimmy Panetta (D-California), Liz Cheney (R-Wyoming), and Ruben Gallego
          (D-Arizona). This act reinforces the Executive Order announced the prior month by President
          Donald Trump adding Huawei and other Chinese telecom providers to the Department of Commerce
          entity list. It would require Congressional approval for the Department of Commerce to
          remove Huawei from the list, and is likely a reaction to the meeting between Donald Trump
          and Xi Jinping at the G20 Summit. The bill would also allow Congress to bar sales from Huawei
          to American companies after they have been granted a waiver.
        </p>
        <p>
          There has been recent pushback against this legislation by several large laptop and
          cellphone manufacturers who claim that in order to remain competitive they need to be able
          to sell to Huawei. They also claim these sales would not raise security concerns, as they
          are not for telecom equipment, and that the security issues stem from Huawei equipment
          entering the country, not U.S. equipment leaving it.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-10 max-w-2xl text-sm text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">
    Full sample, including supply-chain diagrams and source citations, available on request.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-12 border-t border-neutral-200 pt-6 text-sm text-neutral-500 dark:border-neutral-800 dark:text-neutral-400">
    <a href="/writing" class="underline hover:text-neutral-900 dark:hover:text-neutral-100">← Back to Writing Samples</a>
  </p>
</Layout>
